# {{IMPLEMENTER_AGENT}} repository role

## Coordinator-owned local staging qualification

After focused checks, current hosted fast checks and supervisor/Duo review, merge under existing authority; the existing implementer coordinator must then automatically launch and track the full local qualification command on the exact merged integration SHA. Do not wait for an owner-run command or treat hosted fast PASS as full qualification. Automatic post-merge integration/release-branch and tag-push pipelines are disabled, including lightweight checks. Feature MRs retain hosted fast checks; owner-selected RC promotion MRs carry required full hosted release qualification. Separately authorized diagnostic/provider runs remain distinct. Final acceptance still needs actual local full proof and the Goal's other gates.

A ready authorized successor may implement in isolation during that post-merge local run. Respect shared locks, exact inputs and predecessor-owned repairs; predecessor final ACCEPT still gates successor integration/final acceptance. Merged-SHA qualification uses its frozen checkout and is not rebased by development-branch fetch/rebase rules. Preserve explicitly exempt documentation-only work.

{{IMPLEMENTER_AGENT}} owns implementation planning, implementation, tests and validation, and Result reporting.
{{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} owns the roadmap, Goals, Specs, Plan review, acceptance and progress. [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md)
is the shared governance; follow it in full. This file adds no separate governance model.

## Required reading

Before planning or implementation, read AGENTS.md, this file, {{CANONICAL_SOURCES}}, the
[budget policy](management/BUDGET_POLICY.md), the release [ROADMAP](management/{{RELEASE}}/ROADMAP.md) and
[PROGRESS](management/{{RELEASE}}/PROGRESS.md), then the active Goal's Goal, Spec, Plan and Result. Formats:
[roles and lifecycle](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md).

## Workflow

Check [owner mandate/phase/stop bounds](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md#bounded-specification-authority-and-owner-stop-boundaries)
before continuation. A ready Spec/approved Plan never overrides a stop or grants
new specification authority. Do not begin successor lookahead past the endpoint;
clarify ambiguous stops and preserve work at safe pauses.

The supervisor automatically dispatches provisional next-task planning when a
predecessor implementation MR first opens (drafts included), with a ready Goal/Spec.
Do not wait for CI/Duo or merge; planning-only/placeholder/probe MRs do not qualify. Check existing planner ownership and
record pending dependencies as provisional; inspection/Plan artifacts only.
This does not release implementation. Follow
[planning policy](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md#automatic-planning-after-integration).

After [supervisor reassessment/release](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md#automatic-reassessment-and-execution-release)
closes start gates, begin/resume isolated scoped implementation during predecessor
post-merge local full qualification without another routine owner prompt. Final predecessor ACCEPT gates
successor integration/final acceptance. A Plan/merge/green pipeline alone is not
release; material Plan updates require review and explicit holds remain.

Use [mechanical-correction authority](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md#autonomous-mechanical-corrections)
for released in-scope selector/fixture/pin/catalog and harness/import fixes: diagnose,
preserve assertions, record focused evidence and continue without another approval
round. Scope/security/contracts, unapproved schema/constraints and native/budget
stops remain; no change to final review or required CI.

For a reply to a direct owner prompt, omit the `COORDINATOR_STATUS` footer unless the owner explicitly asks to relay that reply to the supervisor. This exception applies to the owner-facing reply only: autonomous supervisor handoffs still use the status marker, and required review submissions, failure records and blocker escalation remain due. Omitting a footer does not guarantee observer silence; existing legacy-prose/native-question detection is unchanged.

As coordinator, finish autonomous supervisor handoffs with a plain final status line:
`COORDINATOR_STATUS: working | TASK-ID | Brief current status.`
Pending decisions (`question`) and review handoffs (`review-ready`) take priority over
another task's routine work; name the actionable task. Use `working` only when no handoff is pending.
Choose the truthful class and follow the complete
[response convention](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#coordinator-final-status-line).
The marker carries no approval authority; use `review-ready` for submitted work, not `completed`.

For failed-batch/correction handoffs, use the
[compact repair checklist](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#compact-repair-handoff),
with existing Result/artifact links and focused excerpts rather than full logs.

1. Write the goal-specific Plan in `management/{{RELEASE}}/plans/` unless the Goal explicitly authorizes the direct
   tier. Map every acceptance criterion to changes and evidence. Recommend a split instead of implementing when
   the Goal exceeds reliable context.
2. If the Goal requires a Plan (every Goal unless its Execution Policy explicitly authorizes the direct tier), stop
   for explicit Plan ACCEPT before implementing. Under an explicitly authorized direct tier, implement within the
   authorized Goal/Spec scope without a separate Plan; the Result still records baseline, changed files and focused
   checks, and the diff is reviewed. Never select a lighter tier yourself; security, data and architecture work keeps
   explicit Spec/Plan review and stronger evidence.
3. Implement within scope; create and run tests; diagnose and iterate. Follow
   [validation and integration](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md): reconcile
   fetch and assess before every batch, reconciling relevant or unknown changes; document any permitted
   irrelevant-delta deferral. Reconcile before final qualification/MR; finish, fix, rerun, advance;
   manual feedback before final qualification.
4. Record the Result: exact commands and outcomes (failed, skipped and interrupted included), the AC checklist,
   deviations, the attempt ledger ([budgets](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/budgets.md)) and an
   outstanding-findings checklist.
5. Submit the review-ready Plan or Result to the coordinator and return control. {{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} reviews it
   and relays the decision without another owner prompt; only {{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} accepts.

A relayed owner authorization is attributed to the owner and its messenger, and it never overrides a native
permission rejection; report the rejected action and reason rather than retrying through another route.

Corrections fix only the named findings and append to the Result. The same material issue after two cycles returns
to the supervisor for reassessment.

## Coordinator, workers and naming

1. `0-agent-<id>` belongs to the single active coordinator only.
2. One coordinator is active, verified by discovery and the owner-selected pin; never create another for an
   unknown result.
3. Names grant no authority; discovery, the pin and recorded ownership do.
4. Prefer `<task-id>-agent-<agent-id>-<role>` under `.claude/worktrees/` when natively supported.
5. Record task/role and preferred path if supported; otherwise use native isolated-worker creation.
6. Before task writes, verify actual path, branch, HEAD, owner and task/role. Unsafe isolation or wrong ownership
   stops; the default generated name alone never does.
7. Unsupported task/role naming is a Claude harness bug/limitation: continue with its native `agent-<id>` worktree,
   recording the mapping without another exception, approval or operator prompt. No permission/lock bypass.
8. Never relocate beneath a live process; relocation defers unless every precondition is positively verified.
9. A permitted relocation is `git worktree move`, verification, same-UUID restart and pin reconciliation.

Full gate: [coordination](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md). Delegate exploration,
review, test analysis or isolated non-overlapping work only; never product or architecture authority, scope changes
or final integration. Workers share the Goal's attempt allowance.

## Effort, budgets and escalation

The coordinator defaults to {{COORDINATOR_EFFORT}} for orchestration, status and handoffs and to {{PLANNING_EFFORT}}
when it plans; planning workers default to {{PLANNING_EFFORT}} and implementation workers to
{{IMPLEMENTATION_EFFORT}}. Configure worker effort explicitly where supported, remind the operator, verify actual
or inherited values and record unverifiable effort as unknown. The owner may raise complex debugging or security
work to xhigh. The default budget class is {{DEFAULT_BUDGET}}; never extend or reset it
yourself. Stop and escalate architecture contradictions, scope expansion, security-model changes, destructive
migrations, public contract changes, new major infrastructure or vendors and irreversible data-model decisions.

## Model-aware effort override — 2026-10-15

Default to Fable 5.1 high for planning and test corrections, including failure
diagnosis and corrective test/fix work; use xhigh selectively for especially
hard work. Default to Opus 5.5 high for implementation, routine test execution
and coordination. If the owner selects Opus 5.5 for planning or test
corrections/diagnosis, use xhigh; routine implementation, test execution and
coordination stay high. This overrides generic role-effort defaults for those
models/phases. Honor owner choices, verify actual settings or report unknown,
and apply only at the next phase/safe boundary without restarting completed work,
interrupting a batch or resetting budgets. Prose does not configure the runtime.


## Fable weekly-limit fallback — owner direction, 2026-10-15

When Fable's weekly allowance is confirmed exhausted, automatically use Opus 5.5
**xhigh for planning and test-failure diagnosis/corrections** until Fable resets.
Opus implementation, ordinary test execution/reporting and coordination remain
**high**. This standing fallback needs no repeat owner approval; honor an explicit
owner override. Record the limit, fallback model/effort and actual setting (or
unknown) in the existing Result/dispatch record. A generic tool error is not
proof of a model limit.

Use supported controls at the next safe boundary, preserving the existing task,
artifacts, checkout, bindings, reviews and attempt count. Do not interrupt a
running validation batch, redo completed work, reset budgets or weaken gates.
If native switching is unavailable or refused, report the blocker once; do not
bypass permissions. Once Fable's allowance reset is verified, return to its normal
model/effort defaults at the next safe phase boundary, without restarting work or
repeatedly probing the allowance. This is model selection only, not scope,
execution-release or budget-extension authority.


Use the shared [validation economy](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#validation-economy-and-recurring-failures),
[automatic routine transitions](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/roles-lifecycle.md#continue-routine-transitions-without-owner-nudges),
[concise messages](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#concise-operational-messages) and
[closeout receipt](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/closeout-risks.md#routine-closeout-receipt).
Preserve explicit owner limits and native permissions; reuse existing records/tools.

## Reuse existing coverage in Plans

Inspect relevant existing tests first. In the normal Plan/AC mapping cite test paths
or check IDs as reuse/extend/new, with the gap or distinct boundary for new tests.
Prefer existing fixtures; no cloned suite or new check merely for this Goal. Keep
required regressions, local/hosted/security coverage and current evidence binding.
See [planning coverage reuse](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#reuse-existing-coverage-during-planning).

At handoffs/closeout or when old conversation history dominates context, follow
[coordinator context compaction](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#coordinator-context-compaction-at-safe-boundaries).
Preserve durable state, session/observer identity and running batches; no per-turn compaction or new monitor.
Use [bounded worker handoffs](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#worker-context-and-phase-handoffs)
when obsolete context warrants them; default checkpoint target **300,000 active context tokens**,
upper handoff threshold **350,000**. Prepare concise transfer state around 300k at a safe boundary;
checkpointing alone does not force immediate rotation or review. Preserve budgets/evidence;
no full-transcript fork, mandatory per-phase rotation or test restart.

Use [targeted reruns and attributable coverage](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#targeted-reruns-and-attributable-coverage-default) by default:
rerun failed cases and affected dependencies, reuse unchanged passes, preserve
failed/canceled history, and overlap frozen-source review with independent tests.
Keep required hosted/atomic evidence and final acceptance gates.

Local validation starts with [focused executable selections](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#focused-selection-before-the-first-run),
including first/final runs. Check actual command scope; broad suites require a
recorded technical reason and supervisor disposition before launch, not convenience.
Reuse approved exceptions without repeated prompts; mandatory hosted gates remain.

Apply [early risk review and short correction loops](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#early-risk-review-and-short-correction-loops)
inside existing planning/review: changed trust boundaries, risky slice, caller/pin
inventory and consolidated corrections. No extra stage, framework or active-work restart.

For [coordinator checkpoints and retirement](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#coordinator-checkpoints-and-retirement),
use 300k active context for checkpointing and 350k for compaction-or-handoff
assessment, not a hard kill. Prefer supported compaction. Replace only with
recorded creation authority, verified native cutover and explicit observer rebind;
preserve active agents, budgets and evidence. Documentation alone does not
implement automatic replacement or retire a live coordinator.

The [supervisor continuity policy](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#supervisor-session-continuity)
also applies when Claude is supervisor. Use native context limits/compaction,
recorded transition exceptions, explicit delegate disposition and observer rebind;
never create a second supervisor or retire an active session solely from age.


## Mandatory task-acceptance cleanup — owner direction, 2026-10-17

At every final task ACCEPT, immediately remove all finished task-owned worktrees
and temporary resources under the [mandatory cleanup policy](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/closeout-risks.md#mandatory-cleanup-at-every-task-acceptance).
Unlock finished agent-tool worktrees; preserve unique commits and dirty/untracked
work before force removal when needed. Clean task-owned containers, unused images
(including attributable untagged IDs), volumes and disposable evidence without
another routine approval. Keep active coordinator/successor/preview resources and
specific recorded retention/native-permission holds. Verify a compact receipt;
no silent lock/dirty-work deferral, blanket host prune or extra test campaign.


## Private files for new tasks — 2026-10-17

Use the main integration checkout's ignored `.local/{evidence,secrets,tmp}/` for
new task-private files, shared across worker worktrees. Follow the [local storage
policy](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/adoption.md#repository-local-private-files): task-scoped paths, private permissions, Git/build/upload exclusions
and separate evidence/secret cleanup. Existing tasks keep current paths through
acceptance cleanup; do not migrate active bindings or native session state.

## Repository-scoped Claude permissions

Follow [adoption policy](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/adoption.md#repository-scoped-claude-permissions):
track shared `.claude/settings.json`; ignore repository-specific personal
`.claude/settings.local.json` and worker checkouts. Home settings are intentionally
cross-project except native classifier configuration requiring that scope.
Verify actual worker/service-root loading; rules do not grant
task authority or waive native gates. Preserve existing configuration on adoption.

## Mandatory short owner reports

Routine owner-facing updates MUST be at most 80 words and three short bullets
(or one short paragraph), plus a status marker only when required for a supervisor handoff. Report only the
new outcome, next action and any decision/blocker requiring attention. No verbose
reports, task rosters, repeated history, hashes, policy recaps or unchanged gates.
Routine acknowledgments, dispatch receipts and unchanged waiting states get no
separate report. Put evidence, inventories and full handbacks in the existing
Result/review and link it; do not paste them into chat. Detailed chat is allowed
only when the owner explicitly asks for detail. A failure/decision must remain
clear within the limit; do not hide it in a link. Preserve truthful uncertainty,
source/final-ACCEPT distinctions and the exact status-marker syntax. This hard
default supersedes earlier guidance to preserve detailed status prose or avoid
fixed-length reports; it changes no technical, native-permission or review gate.

See [operational messages](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/coordination.md#concise-operational-messages).


## Permission failures and focused validation

Distinguish project tool permissions from native classifier configuration using
[adoption guidance](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/adoption.md#tool-permissions-and-classifier-rules).
Keep classifier changes explicitly owner-selected, never bundled security-rule
removals or machine/task-specific grants. Verify effective configuration and
actual approved operations; a native refusal is not cleared by documentation.
Apply [first-failure intervention](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#first-failure-requires-intervention--2026-10-18)
and [resource-based locking](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/validation-integration.md#isolated-checks-and-shared-resource-locks).
Diagnose the first CI failure/refusal and test a correction; do not repeatedly run
unchanged known-failing source or dispatch the same blocked operation elsewhere.

Apply the owner-adopted [permission change handoff](.agents/skills/sdlc-management/references/adoption.md#permission-change-handoff):
the coordinator proposes exact rules, the designated editor installs and reports
the settings delta, and the coordinator verifies effective loading plus actual
native operation before continuing. Use recorded standing authority for routine
approved-scope changes; do not require repeated owner approval or assume installation
proves success. Keep the chosen roles explicit without requiring another supervisor.

## Optional Claude supervisor

The owner may choose Claude Code as supervisor. ChatGPT/Codex then contributes only Work Spec writing and architecture reviews; Claude owns the remaining supervision responsibilities through the existing workflow. This overrides default role assignments only when selected and recorded by the owner; it creates no second supervisor, routine ChatGPT approval gate, session restart or active-task rebind. Major decisions remain owner-owned.


## Owner-selected RC auto-merge — 2026-10-19

The owner may explicitly enable auto-merge for a named RC promotion MR or a clearly recorded scope of RC MRs. Manual promotion remains the default when no such instruction exists. This owner instruction authorizes queuing promotion before CI finishes; once every required full-qualification job succeeds, the provider may merge without another owner prompt. Record the owner instruction, MR, immutable source SHA and selected mode. Before arming, verify the tag/source identity, required complete pipeline, non-squash merge, and conflict-free prospective target tree equal to the RC tree; source-pin the request. Preserve target-movement monitoring and post-merge parent/tree verification without claiming an atomic target lock. Retain and independently verify full project/pipeline/job/artifact proof before claiming qualified release. No failed, skipped, optional or partial coverage counts as qualification. Do not fabricate manual-mode proof or change API snapshots to satisfy a verifier. A manual-only helper's refusal of queued/merged MRs is a mode limitation, not a passing auto-mode proof; use truthful independent verification or a separately reviewed mode-aware adapter. Final release tagging remains a separate owner decision. Feature-MR auto-merge defaults alone never authorize an RC-to-release merge.


## Optional RC Duo review and planned follow-ups — 2026-10-19

RC promotion MRs may use Duo as optional advisory review; Duo is not a required promotion approval gate. Triage every actionable finding and record its disposition in the existing release Result/review. Changes arising from that review must be scheduled and planned as bounded follow-up work: identify the responsible Goal/task or backlog item, intended milestone, scope, acceptance criteria and required focused validation under normal authority. Do not leave actionable recommendations only in an MR comment or silently claim them fixed. Do not change the code or move/recreate an existing RC tag to satisfy a review. If a follow-up changes the release candidate or must ship before promotion, integrate it through the normal staging workflow, then cut and fully qualify a new RC tag under the applicable release authorization. The owner may instead retain a documented prerelease follow-up while the current RC proceeds. Preserve ordinary feature-MR Duo requirements, required full RC CI, security/owner dispositions, selected auto/manual merge mode and the separate final-tag decision. This policy creates no automatic RC2, unbounded repair mandate or new tracker.


## Owner-waived canceled RC qualification — 2026-10-19

The owner may explicitly cancel a named RC promotion MR's pipeline and direct that cancellation to satisfy the current RC promotion gate, treating it as success for the workflow decision only. Record an owner-authorized qualification waiver with the RC/tag/source SHA, MR/pipeline IDs, actual canceled/partial/failed job states, any known findings and the owner's instruction. Never report the canceled pipeline or unexecuted tests as PASS, fabricate green evidence, erase prior failures or claim complete qualification. This exception is specific to the named candidate/pipeline; it is not the default cancellation policy for feature MRs or other RCs. Schedule a follow-up immutable RC tag to run the complete required suite, carrying forward unresolved findings and the qualification debt in the existing release Result/backlog/Plan. The follow-up RC's full suite is required unless the owner separately changes that instruction. The owner may choose this waiver alongside the recorded auto/manual promotion mode; provider auto-merge still depends on the provider's actual cancellation behavior. If existing provider rules prevent merging a canceled pipeline, report that concrete limitation rather than changing project-wide protections, manufacturing a success job or treating a queue as a completed merge. No pipeline is canceled merely because this option is documented, and final release tagging remains separately owner-controlled.
